\details{
\strong{Method}

Procrustes analysis (Gower 1975; Gower & Dijksterhuis 2004) centres \code{ord}
(\eqn{Y}) and \code{target} (\eqn{X}), both \eqn{n \times k}, and finds the
orthogonal matrix \eqn{A} (a rotation, possibly with a reflection) and
the dilation \eqn{c} that minimise
\deqn{m^2 = \| X - c Y A \|^2}
(the sum of squared differences). With \eqn{X^\top Y = U \Sigma V^\top}
(singular value decomposition), \eqn{A = V U^\top}, as in SciPy's
\code{orthogonal_procrustes()}, and
\eqn{c = \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma) / \mathrm{tr}(Y^\top Y)}, or 1 when
\code{dilate = FALSE}.

The ordinary (non-symmetric) \eqn{m^2} depends on the scales of \code{ord}
and \code{target}, and on which one is rotated. With \code{symmetric = TRUE}, both
are first scaled to unit sum of squares, so that
\eqn{m^2 = 1 - \mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)^2} lies between 0 and 1 and is the
same either way round: SciPy's \code{scipy.spatial.procrustes()} "disparity".
\code{\link[=procrustes_test]{procrustes_test()}} reports \eqn{r = \sqrt{1 - m^2}}, this symmetric
Procrustes correlation.

Rotation can't make up for a different number of axes: compare the same
number from each ordination, as the default \code{axes} does.

\strong{Differences from vegan::procrustes()}
\itemize{
\item The argument order: \code{procrustes(ord, target)} rotates \code{ord} onto
\code{target}, as \code{vegan::procrustes(target, ord)} (vegan's \code{procrustes(X, Y)} rotates \code{Y} onto \code{X}).
\item \code{dilate} is vegan's \code{scale}.
\item By default, the axes both have in common are used; vegan pads the one
with fewer axes with zeros. Give \code{axes} to do the same.
\item \code{ord} and \code{target} may be \code{dist} objects, and their samples are
matched by name.
\item The result is the rotated ordination itself, with the rest as
attributes. \code{plot()} draws it with an arrow from each sample to its
place in \code{target} (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}); there is no \code{fitted()}
method.
}

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::procrustes()} takes its arguments the other way round (see
above). When both packages are attached, the one attached last masks the
other: call \code{ecodive::procrustes()} to be sure.
